config SECURITY_PKM
	bool "PKM — Peios Kernel Module"
	depends on SECURITY
	depends on RUST
	depends on 64BIT
	depends on STRICT_DEVMEM
	depends on MODULE_SIG_FORCE
	depends on !SECURITY_SELINUX
	depends on !SECURITY_APPARMOR
	depends on !SECURITY_SMACK
	depends on !SECURITY_TOMOYO
	select CRYPTO_MLDSA
	help
	  PKM is the Peios Kernel Module used for the KACS slow track.
	  The current slow-track tree contains the bounded slow-track
	  scaffold plus the first narrow public token-open surface.
	  Broader syscall, handle, and enforcement behavior is still added
	  deliberately slice by slice rather than carried forward implicitly.

	  KACS must be the sole MAC LSM. Conflicting MAC LSMs (SELinux,
	  AppArmor, SMACK, TOMOYO) are rejected at Kconfig level.
	  Non-MAC LSMs (landlock, lockdown, yama, integrity) are accepted.

	  If unsure, say N.

	  KACS also requires STRICT_DEVMEM and MODULE_SIG_FORCE. These are
	  defense-in-depth prerequisites for PIP: unrestricted /dev/mem and
	  unsigned kernel modules both bypass process-isolation guarantees by
	  entering the kernel trust boundary directly.

config SECURITY_PKM_FIRMWARE_SIG_ENFORCE
	bool "Refuse firmware that does not carry a PeiosTcb PIP signature"
	depends on SECURITY_PKM
	default n
	help
	  KACS verifies the PIP signature (security.peios.sig) of every file
	  the kernel reads as firmware and requires the PeiosTcb tier. With
	  this option the load is refused (-EPERM) when that fails; without
	  it the verdict is logged and traced (kacs:kacs_firmware_load) and
	  the load proceeds. Either way the boot command line can override
	  the choice for one boot with kacs_fwsig=enforce or kacs_fwsig=log.

	  Leave this off until every firmware package in the release is
	  signed; a missed blob under enforcement is a device that silently
	  stops probing. PEI-493.

config SECURITY_PKM_KUNIT
	bool "PKM KUnit scaffold"
	depends on SECURITY_PKM
	depends on KUNIT
	help
	  Build the minimal PKM-owned KUnit scaffold.

	  This option is only for kernel-resident glue that Cargo tests cannot
	  exercise. Pure `kacs-core` semantics remain owned by the Rust
	  userspace/Cargo test suite.

	  If unsure, say N.
